// show_existing_frame runtime, bit-exact vs the SW oracle. The SW golden
// (reference state at each show_existing event + the keyframe-case parse result)
// was captured once and embedded below, so this test no longer depends on the
// SW oracle at runtime.
//
// The stream is a real 16-frame 64x64 4:2:0 8-bit AV1 OBU produced by
// aomenc 3.12.1 with automatic alt-ref frames (an ARF is coded show_frame=0
// then re-displayed later by a show_existing_frame header). Encoder:
//   aomenc --obu --cpu-used=4 --end-usage=q --cq-level=32 --limit=16 --passes=1
//     --kf-max-dist=100 --lag-in-frames=16 --auto-alt-ref=1 --enable-cdef=1
//     --enable-restoration=0
// It carries two show_existing_frame OBUs (both re-display a non-key ARF, so
// refresh_frame_flags = 0). No film grain is present in this stream.
//
// What is verified end to end:
//  * HarborFrameHeaderParse parses the show_existing_frame header bit-exact:
//    show_existing_frame flag, frame_to_show_map_idx, refresh_frame_flags
//    (the KEY_FRAME-forces-0xff rule), order_hint and frame_type pulled from
//    the shown slot's saved metadata, reaching done without unsupported.
//  * The parsed frame_to_show_map_idx + refresh_frame_flags drive HarborDpb
//    (the DPB data plane) to select and copy the stored reference: the shown
//    pixels are bit-exact (0 diffs) vs the frame the SW oracle displays, with
//    all 8 slots populated by distinct decoded frames so a wrong index would
//    return the wrong pixels.
//  * A synthetic KEY_FRAME-in-slot case exercises the 0xff-refresh branch and
//    the all-8-slot refresh copy, with the golden taken from a SW
//    parseFrameHeader over a keyframe-populated RefState.

